This is a Coq formalization of a simplified version of the Ur programming
language, and a machine-checked proof of its core soundness properties.

What is proved (Semantics.v), against a denotational semantics (cDen/eDen):

  subs_correct      substitution is meaning-preserving
  deq_correct       the type-equality judgment is sound
  disj_correct      the row-DISJOINTNESS judgment is sound
  cut_disjoint      cutting a name out of a disjoint row preserves disjointness
  name_eq_dec_refl  decidable name equality is reflexive on the nose

Disjointness is the load-bearing one for Ur's record calculus: it is what makes
a `[fs1 ~ fs2]` constraint mean anything, i.e. what rules out two field sets
silently aliasing.

Assumptions: exactly one axiom, `ext_eq` (functional extensionality, Axioms.v).
No `Admitted`, no `admit`.

Build and kernel-check:

  make          # coqc via coq_makefile
  make check    # coqchk -- re-checks the proof terms in Coq's trusted kernel,
                # independently of the tactic engine

Tested with Coq 8.19.2. (It was previously pinned to 8.3pl2 from 2011 and had
been unbuildable for about a decade; reviving it needed one tactic rename,
`elimtype False` -> `exfalso`.) CI builds AND kernel-checks it on every push,
so it cannot silently rot again.
